      en: "The Boma Plateau in eastern South Sudan (Jonglei State) rises to an average elevation of 1,000 m, with peaks at 1,200 m. It is part of Boma National Park (established 1986, 22,800 km²). The plateau hosts one of Africa's largest mammal migrations: up to 200,000 white-eared kob (Kobus kob leucotis) move seasonally between Boma and the Sudd. Other species: reticulated giraffe, African elephant, and zebra. Annual rainfall: 500–700 mm. The human population is sparse, mainly Murle and Jie pastoralists. The migration is threatened by hunting and habitat loss. The park is also home to the endangered painted hunting dog (Lycaon pictus).",
